 "is_vocab": false,
 "stimulus_html": "For the following problems, recall that if we flip a coin at least 20 times, the distribution of the number of heads is approximately normal with mean equal to half the number of flips and standard deviation equal to half of the square root of the number of flips.",
 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 528663,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Suppose we flip a coin 120 times. What are the mean and standard deviation of the corresponding distribution of heads?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Since the number of heads is distributed normally, it can be shown that the mean of <i>n</i> flips is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2}\"></span>and the standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2}\"></span> as long as <span data-math=\"n \\geq {\\text{ }}20\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Since <i>n</i> is 120, the mean is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2} = \\frac{{120}}{2} = 60\"></span>.</p>\n<p>The standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {120} }}{2} \\approx 5.477\"></span>.</p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "<span data-math=\"\\mu  = 60\">\\mu  = 60</span>; <span data-math=\"\\sigma  = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {120}  \\approx 5.477\">\\sigma  = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {120}  \\approx 5.477</span>"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 528664,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Let’s say you flip 70 heads in 120 flips. At what percentile would that fall?",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Since the number of heads is distributed normally, it can be shown that the mean of <i>n</i> flips is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2}\"></span>and the standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2}\"></span> as long as <span data-math=\"n \\geq {\\text{ }}20\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Since <i>n</i> is 120, the mean is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2} = \\frac{{120}}{2} = 60\"></span>.</p>\n<p>The standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {120} }}{2} \\approx 5.477\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Use your mean and standard deviation and the technology of Google Sheets.</p>\n<p>In an empty cell of Google Sheets, use the function NORM.DIST.</p>\n<p>NORM.DIST(your data value, your mean, your standard deviation, TRUE)</p>\n<p>Using your data: =NORM.DIST(70, 60, 5.477,TRUE)</p>\n<p>You will see the decimal version of the percentile: 0.9660610881</p>\n<p>Convert the decimal to an approximate percentage: the 96.6<sup>th</sup> percentile</p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "96.6th"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  },
  {
   "id": 528665,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "How many heads would be at the 30th percentile? Round to the nearest whole number.",
   "answers": [],
   "hints": [],
   "formats": [
    "free-response"
   ],
   "combo_choices": [],
   "collaborator_solutions": [
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "summary",
     "content_html": "<p>Since the number of heads is distributed normally, it can be shown that the mean of <i>n</i> flips is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2}\"></span>and the standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2}\"></span> as long as <span data-math=\"n \\geq {\\text{ }}20\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Since <i>n</i> is 120, the mean is <span data-math=\"\\frac{n}{2} = \\frac{{120}}{2} = 60\"></span>.</p>\n<p>The standard deviation is <span data-math=\"\\frac{{\\sqrt n }}{2} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {120} }}{2} \\approx 5.477\"></span>.</p>\n<p>Use your mean and standard deviation and the technology of Google Sheets.</p>\n<p>When your data is normally distributed and you know the percentile, mean, and standard deviation, you can find the data value with the NORM.INV function.</p>\n<p>NORM.INV(percentile as a decimal, mean, standard deviation)</p>\n<p>In an empty cell in Google Sheets: =NORM.INV(0.30, 60, 5.477)</p>\n<p>What you see: 57.12785839</p>\n<p>Rounded version: 57</p>\n"
    },
    {
     "images": [],
     "solution_type": "detailed",
     "content_html": "57"
    }
   ],
   "community_solutions": []
  }
 ],
